            "width": 37.98882681564246,
            "height": 8.830022075055187,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><span style=\"font-size:1.5em;\"><strong>INFORMATION TEXT (Part 2)</strong></span></p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "55aaf4d3-f7e3-4a3e-9196-01a6f7474f94"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          },
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582782,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Paul's Teachings</strong><br>\nPaul's teachings emphasized salvation through faith in Christ rather than through the observance of Jewish law. This was a radical shift from the prevailing Jewish thought at the time and played a key role in distinguishing Christianity as a separate faith. Paul's letters to the early churches form a significant part of the New Testament and are foundational to Christian theology.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Challenges and Persecution</strong><br>\nThroughout his missionary work, Paul faced considerable opposition and persecution. He was frequently imprisoned, beaten, and faced hostility both from the Jewish community and Roman authorities. Despite these challenges, he remained steadfast in his mission, using his Roman citizenship to appeal for a trial in Rome.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Writings of Paul</strong><br>\nPaul is credited with writing 13 of the 27 books in the New Testament, known as the Pauline epistles. These letters offer insight into his thoughts, theological teachings, and instructions for the early Christian communities. They deal with a variety of issues ranging from church organization to moral living.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "3b13867b-feab-4e2e-bfa2-fcc82e4dbff1"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          }
        ],
